---
import CruiserHullGallery from "../../../components/studies/CruiserHullGallery.astro";
import CruiserSlotSketch from "../../../components/studies/CruiserSlotSketch.astro";
import IronKeelRefinements from "../../../components/studies/IronKeelRefinements.astro";
import LongBevelPlacement from "../../../components/studies/LongBevelPlacement.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "heavy-cruiser-slots");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="problem">
		<h2 id="problem">01 / Overwhelming power in a slim hull</h2>
		<p>
			The Fighter says danger; this cruiser should say overwhelming power. Armor
			does not require bulk. The owner's naval battleship reference sets the
			direction: a long, purposeful body that looks powerful and streamlined,
			with a sense of protection and weight rather than fighter-like agility.
			Imperial light battleships are a mood reference, without the triangular
			wedge.
		</p>
		<p>
			Start with the eight existing mounts: two mirrored side engines, three
			hull slots and three aft slots. Arrange those together with two or three
			cockpit windows, then develop the low-poly armor around them. A repeated
			window rhythm could make the bridge read as a larger crewed vessel;
			whether it survives at flight size remains to be examined.
		</p>
		<p>
			This direction replaces the opening emphasis on a “substantial” body:
			power, protection and slenderness now guide the spatial study. The axial
			hull layout is now carried forward from owner review in 02.1; aft
			placement and window count remain open. Handling, balance and the final
			chassis name follow separately; the production ship is still disabled.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / Slots and bridge before armor surfaces</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / A long battery or paired shoulders?</h3>
		<p>
			Read these from bow to stern. A places all three hull mounts along the
			spine, giving the deck a long repeated rhythm. B puts two abreast behind
			the forward mount, concentrating equipment across the shoulders. In both,
			the bridge sits behind the hull fittings and ahead of the engine section.
			Compare the layouts first, then the two- and three-window bridge versions.
		</p>
		<div class="comparison">
			{([2, 3] as const).map((windows) => (
				<>
					<figure>
						<figcaption>
							A{windows} / Axial battery · {windows} windows
						</figcaption>
						<CruiserSlotSketch
							layout="spine"
							windows={windows}
							label={`Axial battery with ${windows} bridge windows; three hull mounts in a line`}
						/>
					</figure>
					<figure>
						<figcaption>
							B{windows} / Paired shoulders · {windows} windows
						</figcaption>
						<CruiserSlotSketch
							layout="shoulders"
							windows={windows}
							label={`Paired shoulders with ${windows} bridge windows; one forward hull mount and two abreast`}
						/>
					</figure>
				</>
			))}
		</div>
		<p class="legend">
			E = required side engine · H1–3 = gun/utility · A1–3 = engine/gun/utility.
			Dark panes are cockpit windows; outlined boxes show empty mount
			footprints.
		</p>
		<p>
			Study fixture: production mount identities and counts, proposed positions,
			one fixed hull envelope and bridge width across all four drawings. Armor
			lines only suggest protective edges; these are spatial sketches, not final
			facets or the production renderer. They do not establish fitted-part
			clearance, flight-scale readability, collision or handling.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner review: the axial hull arrangement in A2 is the direction to carry
			forward. The bridge behind the fittings does not work; the cockpit belongs
			clearly at the front. Two panes are retained only as a comparison setting,
			not a settled window count. These first sketches remain as the evidence
			that exposed the cockpit-placement problem.
		</p>
		<h3 id="aft-triangles">02.2 / Bow cockpit and triangular aft mounts</h3>
		<p>
			The cockpit now leads the ship, followed by H1, H2 and H3 in a straight
			line. Below, only the aft mounts change: a row, the center moved forward,
			or the outer pair moved forward. The hull envelope, side engines, mount
			sizes and two-pane cockpit are identical in all three.
		</p>
		<div class="comparison aft-comparison">
			<figure>
				<figcaption>C / Straight aft row</figcaption>
				<CruiserSlotSketch
					layout="spine"
					windows={2}
					bowCockpit
					aft="row"
					label="C: bow cockpit, three axial hull slots and a straight aft row"
				/>
				<p>A reference: three fittings terminate at the same stern edge.</p>
			</figure>
			<figure>
				<figcaption>D / Center forward · pair behind</figcaption>
				<CruiserSlotSketch
					layout="spine"
					windows={2}
					bowCockpit
					aft="center-forward"
					label="D: bow cockpit, axial hull slots, center aft mount forward of the outer pair"
				/>
				<p>
					The pair brackets a recessed central engine lane. This groups the
					stern around two shoulders.
				</p>
			</figure>
			<figure>
				<figcaption>E / Pair forward · center behind</figcaption>
				<CruiserSlotSketch
					layout="spine"
					windows={2}
					bowCockpit
					aft="center-back"
					label="E: bow cockpit, axial hull slots, outer aft pair forward of the center mount"
				/>
				<p>
					The center extends the ship's spine to the stern; the pair could sit
					on stepped engine shoulders.
				</p>
			</figure>
		</div>
		<p>
			Dashed lines mark rearward engine centerlines, not rendered exhaust. Both
			triangles keep these lines clear of the other mount footprints, so a
			triangular arrangement makes spatial sense. Neither proves clearance for a
			full engine plume. Forward nozzles also sit over the provisional hull: the
			armor will need an open channel, a stepped stern or an explicit raised
			mount. The hull outline here deliberately stays fixed to expose that
			issue.
		</p>
		<p>
			My initial read is that E strengthens the long, purposeful spine, while D
			concentrates weight in the outer stern pair. These are visual hypotheses;
			actual fittings, facets and flight scale remain the next checks. This
			round does not change thrust or steering.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner direction: carry option D into hull exploration, retaining the bow
			cockpit and axial hull slots. Closer spacing is allowed. This settles the
			arrangement for the next experiment, not its final dimensions or a
			finished ship.
		</p>
		<h3 id="six-hulls">02.3 / Six hulls around the same arrangement</h3>
		<p>
			Six original takes on classic science-fiction warship forms, all elongated
			and all using D. The hull-slot spacing is tightened from 55 to 43 sketch
			units; the aft pair from 38 to 33 units either side of center. The forward
			cockpit, side engines and all eight mounts occupy identical positions
			across the six. Window shapes can change; two panes remain a working
			choice.
		</p>
		<p>
			Compare armor mass and silhouette with the slots visible, then hide them
			to judge the ship itself. These use the production Fighter's flat colors
			and black cockpit, with new study-only polygons. The Fighter below is
			drawn from its current geometry as a style reference, not a size
			comparison.
		</p>
		<CruiserHullGallery />
		<p>
			The sterns leave a channel behind D's center mount. These are hull studies
			with empty slot overlays: fitted parts, plume width, small-scale flight
			readability and handling still need verification. No production ship
			definition has changed.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner review: only 01 / Iron keel is narrowly promising. The other five
			directions are set aside. The first gallery reads too large, its mounts
			too small and far apart, and its cockpit and facets fail the intended
			sleek low-poly character. The gallery is retained as evidence, not an
			accepted design.
		</p>
		<h3 id="compact-keels">02.4 / Six compact Iron Keels</h3>
		<p>
			All six preserve the forward cockpit, three axial hull mounts and D's aft
			triangle. This round uses game drawing units: hull/aft footprints are 9 ×
			11, matching the Fighter's 11 × 9 mounts rotated into this top view. Hull
			centers are 13 units apart, leaving a two-unit gap. Side mounts also use
			the Fighter's footprint. Each cruiser is shown beside the current Fighter
			at exactly the same scale.
		</p>
		<p>
			The cruiser hulls are 85–92 units long versus the Fighter's 73, rather
			than an arbitrarily enlarged illustration. Widths are 42–46 versus 40.
			Those are trial dimensions, not accepted gameplay sizes. Six restrained
			variations replace the earlier broad search: long angular planes, compact
			dark cockpit panes and tightly grouped equipment. Hide the mounts to
			inspect the surfaces.
		</p>
		<IronKeelRefinements />
		<p>
			The cockpit stays at the bow; two small panes are still provisional. These
			drawings use the current Fighter geometry and palette but study-only
			cruiser polygons. Empty mounts show spatial fit; installed parts, engine
			plumes, collision and actual flight scale remain untested. The original
			gallery and D comparison are preserved above.
		</p>
		<p>
			Owner decision: retain 08 / Long bevel as good enough to proceed, with
			explicit dissatisfaction about visual quality. This is the working hull
			for placement exploration, not a claim that its appearance is fully
			successful. The other five refinements are set aside.
		</p>
		<h3 id="long-bevel-placement">02.5 / Long bevel at assembly scale</h3>
		<p>
			The exact 08 hull and cockpit are enlarged below. Select a mount, then
			adjust its position with the sliders (arrow keys work too). Hull mounts
			stay on the spine; side engines and the outer aft pair move symmetrically.
			The center aft mount moves independently, so D's triangle can be
			tightened. Reset restores the preserved 08 arrangement.
		</p>
		<LongBevelPlacement client:load />
		<p>
			Side-angle correction: the earlier overlays reversed the side-engine
			rotation in the bow-up view. This placement view now points the left
			engine inward at +20° and the right at −20°, so their rearward guides
			diverge outward. Increasing the angle increases this outward exhaust
			direction. Earlier sketches retain their original geometry as historical
			evidence.
		</p>
		<p>
			H1–H3 accept guns or utilities. A1–A3 also accept engines; both side
			engines are required and fitted as a pair. In the existing game, one input
			fires its side engine, while both inputs fire both side engines and all
			installed aft engines. Guns currently have no shooting behavior. The
			orange guides indicate rearward engine axes only, not full plume clearance
			or simulated thrust.
		</p>
		<p>
			At the starting placement, the hull mounts have two units between their
			footprints. The side mounts partly overhang the armor; the center aft
			mount sits just ahead of the stern channel. Those are the relationships to
			inspect here. Positions remain a study fixture: fitted-part clearance,
			cockpit separation, engine effects and handling need a later
			production-system check.
		</p>
		<p>
			Next owner proposal: move the side engines forward and consider moving one
			hull mount ahead of the cockpit. The original placement tool above remains
			available.
		</p>
		<h3 id="forward-engines">02.6 / Forward engines and a bow mount</h3>
		<p>
			The screenshot's side-engine settings are preserved here: fore–aft −22.5,
			distance 13, angle 34°. The remaining positions are reconstructed
			approximately from the picture: H1 −14.5, H2 −0.5, H3 12.5; center aft
			30.5, outer pair 37 at ±11. These are trial coordinates, not an accepted
			final layout.
		</p>
		<p>
			The forward engine pair can work spatially: its rearward axes point
			outward, away from the central deck. It will change steering leverage, so
			this is not evidence of good flight handling. Moving H1 to the very front
			gives a clear bow fitting with two deck slots behind the cockpit, while
			keeping the total at eight mounts.
		</p>
		<LongBevelPlacement
			client:load
			forwardTrial
			title="Forward engines and bow-mount comparison"
		/>
		<p>
			Use “Try H1 at bow” to compare the same ship with only H1 moved. A
			full-size mount ahead of the cockpit does not fit entirely within the
			current narrow tip: it needs an armored support or a modest bow change. A
			gun would also need its barrel and firing line checked; guns do not yet
			fire in the game. Exhaust widths and attachment details still need
			inspection with real parts.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted — 30 October 2026.</strong>
			The owner approved the 02.6 direction and requested readiness and
			integration planning. Carry forward 08 / Long bevel, its forward cockpit,
			the forward side-engine pair, H1 ahead of the cockpit, two remaining deck
			mounts and D's aft triangle. The earlier reservations about visual polish
			remain recorded; acceptance does not imply a successful flight test.
		</p>
		<p>
			The fixed integration baseline below captures the displayed bow trial,
			independently of temporary slider edits. Screenshot-derived coordinates
			are implementation starting values; the accepted spatial relationships are
			the design contract.
		</p>
		<div class="placement-table">
			<table>
				<caption>
					Accepted reference: 02.4 variant 08 + 02.6 bow trial · top-view units
				</caption>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Mount</th>
						<th>Across</th>
						<th>Fore–aft</th>
						<th>Rotation</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td>Left / right engine</td>
						<td>−13 / +13</td>
						<td>−22.5</td>
						<td>+34° / −34°</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>H1 · bow</td>
						<td>0</td>
						<td>−45.5</td>
						<td>0°</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>H2 / H3 · deck</td>
						<td>0</td>
						<td>−0.5 / +12.5</td>
						<td>0°</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>A1 / A3 · outer aft</td>
						<td>−11 / +11</td>
						<td>+37</td>
						<td>0°</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>A2 · center aft</td>
						<td>0</td>
						<td>+30.5</td>
						<td>0°</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p>
			Reference footprints: side 9 × 14; hull/aft 9 × 11. Preserve the 08
			facets, two black bow panes and elongated proportions. Bow support is
			required; its final polygon treatment remains provisional.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Feasible with shared geometry adaptations</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Ready to implement as an alpha trial.</strong>
			The existing tank chassis already stores the required two side, three hull
			and three aft fittings. The builder, fitting validation, energy model,
			test flight and engine effects can be reused. No new dependency or backend
			feature is needed. The current production renderer still uses the old
			generic tank hull and mount positions; the study has not re-enabled the
			ship.
		</p>
		<h3>Changes needed before a meaningful flight review</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>One shared cruiser definition.</strong>
				Production mount generation only special-cases the Fighter; add cruiser
				hull, facets, cockpit and explicit mount geometry. Builder hit targets,
				fitted-part drawing and engine emitters must consume those same mounts.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Bow support and actual fittings.</strong>
				H1 extends beyond the old tip and has only a one-unit longitudinal gap
				before the cockpit. Add a small armored support or modest tip widening,
				retaining the approved silhouette. Inspect every compatible part; a rail
				barrel and utility footprint may expose issues an empty rectangle
				cannot.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Correct coordinate conversion.</strong>
				Study coordinates are across/right and aft/down; production uses +x
				forward. Convert (across, aft) to (−aft, across), and convert mount
				orientation consistently. Side engines therefore use sideX 22.5, arm 13
				and angle 34°. Verify left/right signs rather than repeating the earlier
				overlay reversal.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Collision coverage.</strong>
				The generic tank collision radius does not describe this long bow.
				Extend buildCollisionRadius to cover the new hull, bow support and
				relevant part extents at the existing 0.65 drawing scale. A conservative
				circle is feasible but may feel unfair along the narrow sides; assess
				that in flight.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>What can remain provisional in the alpha</h3>
		<p>
			Reuse tank mass 140, existing engine thrust, energy draw and speed limits.
			Derive the side-engine turning lever from the new position and angle;
			retain a provisional response gain rather than borrowing the Fighter's
			2.5× tuning without evidence. Chassis length in the turning calculation
			must be explicitly derived from the chosen local origin and extent, not
			confused with the full 92-unit illustration length. Human flight review
			then judges power, turning responsiveness and collision fairness. Gravity
			and friction are outside this change.
		</p>
		<p>
			Engine plumes already use mounted nozzle positions, but the forward pair
			and compact aft channel need visual clearance checks with all aft engines
			active. The current model keeps aft thrust free of asymmetric steering and
			does not move the center of mass with fittings. This design can use that
			existing convention; it does not establish a new rigid-body model or
			shooting behavior.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Evidence limit:</strong>
			owner approval covers the visual/layout direction. The study controls,
			coordinate updates and prior formatting/type checks establish
			inspectability only. Production fitting clearance, collision, handling and
			flight-scale fidelity are not yet verified. Bow support is an
			implementation task with a review point, not a reason to reopen the whole
			silhouette search.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">
			04 / Integrate the approved cruiser into the builder and flight
		</h2>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Freeze and translate the reference.</strong>
				Preserve 02.4 variant 08 and the accepted coordinate table. Add a typed
				cruiser geometry definition alongside fighter.ts in
				packages/mode-a/src/lib. Preserve flat facets and cockpit polygons;
				incorporate the minimal bow support. If a geometry or configuration data
				contract changes, define its Zod schema before adapting consumers.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Connect rendering, fitting and effects.</strong>
				Update build-renderer.ts: the tank branch in drawBuild, explicit
				buildMounts positions/orientations, nozzle origins and
				buildCollisionRadius. Use the same definition in the builder and
				FlightDeck. Demonstrate empty slots, all compatible fittings and
				all-engine exhaust in /lab/builder. Review bow support and cockpit
				clearance here before treating the drawing as faithful.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Connect chassis calculations.</strong>
				Update the tank entry in schemas/loadout.ts with the approved side
				geometry and a documented length/turn-response convention. Reuse
				build-flight.ts and simulation.ts behavior; adapt only where the
				existing geometry assumptions require it. Compare single-engine and
				paired-engine trials, braking and mixed-engine overspeed using the real
				simulation.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Re-enable selection safely.</strong>
				Update ShipBuilder.svelte to allow Fighter and tank, leaving Scout
				pending. Retain the tank ID and version-1 saved loadouts, including each
				hull/aft slot index, so existing builds survive. Keep the existing Heavy
				Corvette display name for this integration unless separately renamed.
				Custom survival remains unranked and stock scored profiles remain
				unchanged.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Update authoritative guidance.</strong>
				Update docs/specs/ship-builder.md with implemented geometry, enabled
				selection, collision and tuning limitations; update docs/design.md with
				the adopted cruiser reference and canonical Lab destination. Keep target
				behavior distinct from delivered behavior until implementation lands.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Verify and review.</strong>
				Test slot compatibility and saved-build preservation; mount/nozzle
				alignment; left/right turn signs and paired cancellation; energy and
				speed-limit behavior; and collision coverage. Run the repository's pnpm
				verify gates and report every failure or unavailable check. Inspect
				keyboard selection and existing landscape flight touch controls, then
				compare builder and flight rendering with the approved reference. Human
				review separately assesses bow attachment, small-scale readability,
				engine clearance and handling.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Sequencing: shared geometry and bow support → renderer/mounts/collision →
			chassis calculations → selection and documentation → automated gates and
			human flight review. Do not tune away the accepted layout merely to obtain
			a familiar flight feel; document tuning separately and return material
			silhouette changes for review.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Delivery status:</strong>
			implemented locally after the owner's “deliver it” instruction. The shared
			cruiser definition, armored bow support, explicit mounts, nozzle
			positions, collision coverage and builder selection are connected. Saved
			tank IDs and slot indices are preserved.
			<a href="/lab/builder"
				>Review the delivered cruiser in the canonical builder and test
				flight.</a
			>
			Human assessment of fidelity and handling remains pending. This is not a
			deployment claim.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
.placement-table {
	overflow-x: auto;
	margin-block: var(--spacing-md);
}
table {
	width: 100%;
	border-collapse: collapse;
}
th,
td {
	text-align: left;
	padding: var(--spacing-sm);
	border-bottom: var(--flight-border);
}
caption {
	text-align: left;
	margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
}
.comparison {
	display: grid;
	grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
	gap: var(--spacing-md);
}
figure {
	margin: 0;
	padding: var(--spacing-md);
	border: var(--flight-border);
}
figcaption {
	font: var(--font-small) var(--font-flight);
	color: var(--color-acid-lime);
}
.legend {
	margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
	color: var(--color-text-dim);
}
.invitation {
	border-left: 2px solid var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
@media (max-width: 580px) {
	.comparison {
		grid-template-columns: 1fr;
	}
}
</style>
